<h2 id="the-last-mile-of-a-warning">The last mile of a warning</h2><p>Flood forecasting in Bangladesh has improved a great deal. But a forecast is only useful if it reaches the person who has to move their family, livestock and seed stock, and if they trust it enough to act. On the chars, the shifting river islands of the Jamuna and Brahmaputra, that last mile is the hardest part.</p>
<p>Char families are often far from official river gauges, may not have reliable internet, and hear warnings that use place names and water levels that do not match what they see on their own island.</p>
<blockquote>A warning that arrives on time, in the wrong words, is still a warning that arrives too late.</blockquote>
<h2 id="a-low-cost-design-to-test">A low-cost design to test</h2><p>This working paper sets out a design that young engineers on the Delta pipeline have proposed: small solar-powered water-level sensors placed near the community, a low-power radio mesh between them, and short Bangla SMS alerts that refer to local landmarks. The design aims to work without smartphones or mobile data.</p>
<h2 id="what-we-will-measure">What we will measure</h2><p>The field test will ask three questions: how much earlier a family receives a local warning, whether they understand and trust it, and what it costs to install and maintain per village. We will publish the method before the results, and the results whatever they show.</p><p class="tags"><a class="tg" href="{{ route('insights.index') }}?tag=flood">#flood</a><a class="tg" href="{{ route('insights.index') }}?tag=early-warning">#early-warning</a><a class="tg" href="{{ route('insights.index') }}?tag=sensors">#sensors</a></p></article></div></section>
